@node Introduction
@chapter Introduction

@command{fontina} reads the font files already on your disk, records what it finds in
one SQLite file, and answers questions about them: which families you have, which cover
a script or a particular string of text, which are duplicates, which are broken, and
what each one's license permits you to do.

It is built on three promises.

@table @strong
@item It does not touch what is not yours
@command{fontina} never writes to a system font directory and never asks for elevation.
Everything it installs or activates is per-user.

@item Nothing leaves the machine
No network connection, no accounts, no update checks, no crash reporting, no telemetry.
Not disabled by default: absent.

@item Your data stays yours, in formats you can read
One SQLite file for the index, JSON with a published schema for every export, TOML for
config, fontconfig XML on GNU/Linux.  Delete the binary and your fonts, your folders and
your operating system's font registrations are exactly as you left them.
@end table

@command{fontina} is free software.  You may run it for any purpose, study how it works,
change it, and redistribute copies with or without your changes, under the GNU General
Public License version 3 or, at your option, any later version.  @xref{Freedom}, for
what the program can tell you about the freedom of your fonts.

@node Invoking fontina
@chapter Invoking fontina

@example
fontina [--db PATH] COMMAND [OPTIONS] [ARGS...]
@end example

Every command accepts @option{--json}, which prints a stable document described by
@code{fontina schema cli-output} instead of the human-readable form.  A script written
against one release keeps working against the next: that is what the schema is for.

@section Building an index

@table @code
@item fontina scan PATH@dots{}
Index the fonts under one or more directories or files.
@item fontina scan --system
Index the operating system's font directories.
@item fontina stats
Index statistics and recent parse failures.
@end table

@section Searching

@table @code
@item fontina list [QUERY]
List indexed faces.
@item fontina families
The same faces grouped by typographic family name.
@item fontina facets
Count matching faces per weight, width, style, script, license, freedom, vendor, tag,
collection, activation state and source.
@item fontina covers TEXT
Faces whose character map covers every character of @var{TEXT}.
@end table

@noindent
@code{list}, @code{families}, @code{facets} and @code{covers} share one set of filters:
@option{--family}, @option{--variable}, @option{--color}, @option{--italic},
@option{--script}, @option{--license}, @option{--free}, @option{--nonfree},
@option{--freedom}, @option{--weight}, @option{--width}, @option{--vendor},
@option{--tag}, @option{--collection}, @option{--active}, @option{--activation},
@option{--container} and @option{--under}.

@section Activating

Everything here is per-user.  Nothing is written to a system font directory and nothing
asks for elevation.

@table @code
@item fontina activate FACE@dots{}
Make faces visible to other applications, in place.  Persistent for the user unless
@option{--session}.  Exit status 2 when a conflict blocks it.
@item fontina deactivate FACE@dots{}
Undo it.
@item fontina install FACE@dots{}
Copy faces into the per-user font directory; @code{uninstall} removes those copies.
@item fontina conflicts FACE@dots{}
Faces that would clash with these once active: same PostScript name, or same family and
style, already active or living in an OS font directory.
@item fontina activations
Everything fontina has activated or installed.  @code{restore} re-applies the record,
for a login agent or after a reboot.
@item fontina agent install
Have the operating system run @code{restore} when you log in, so activations come back
after a reboot without anyone remembering to ask.  Off until you install it, per-user
everywhere — a systemd user unit, a LaunchAgent, or an entry in your Startup folder —
and removed by @code{fontina agent uninstall}.  Nothing is written outside your home
directory and nothing needs elevation, so it cannot affect anyone else on the machine.
@item fontina watch [PATH@dots{}]
Follow the watched sources and keep the index current until interrupted.  One line per
batch of changes, or one JSON object per line with @option{--json}.
@end table

@section Seeing the fonts

@table @code
@item fontina preview FACE@dots{}
Real, shaped glyphs in the terminal — kitty, iTerm2 or sixel images, or half-blocks
anywhere else — or a PNG.  Shaping is HarfBuzz-grade, so complex scripts are correct
rather than approximated.
@item fontina ui
Browse the index: facets, families, faces, details and previews, keyboard first.
@kbd{s} writes an HTML specimen for what is selected and opens it in your browser ---
the one place to go for what a terminal cannot show honestly: real antialiasing at text
sizes, spacing you can trust, hinting.  @env{BROWSER} is honoured before the desktop's
default, and on a machine with no desktop the file is still written and its path
printed.  @kbd{?} lists every key.
@end table

@section Looking at one font

@table @code
@item fontina info FACE
Everything known about one face, named by index id or by file path.  A path that is not
in the index is parsed on the spot.
@item fontina glyphs FACE
Character coverage by Unicode block.
@item fontina license [FACE@dots{}]
SPDX identifier, freedom verdict and the reason for it, embedding rights, reserved font
names and copyright.
@item fontina check PATH@dots{}
Health checks over a face's metadata.  Exit status 1 if any check reports an error.
@item fontina dupes
Faces that are the same font in several files, matched across containers by a hash of
the name and outline tables.
@end table

@section Getting fonts back out

@table @code
@item fontina css FACE@dots{}
@code{@@font-face} rules, following CSS Fonts Level 4.
@item fontina specimen FACE@dots{}
A self-contained HTML specimen: waterfall, script samples, axis sliders, feature
toggles, glyph map and side-by-side comparison.  The file makes no network requests, so
it works from a local disk, from an archive, and in twenty years.
@item fontina man
Write the man pages, generated from the command definitions themselves so they cannot
drift from @option{--help}.  @code{fontina completions SHELL} does the same for shell
completions.
@item fontina schema [face|collection|cli-output]
A JSON Schema for the metadata model, a collection export, or every type
@option{--json} prints.
@item fontina collection export NAME [FILE]
Write a collection as JSON: the faces in order, each with its family, style, hashes,
path and tags.  It is a list of what you chose, useful to anyone who already has the
same fonts.
@item fontina collection export NAME --bundle DIR
Write the collection @emph{and} the fonts.  @var{DIR} gets a @file{collection.json} and
a @file{fonts/} directory holding a copy of every file the collection names, and every
path in the JSON is relative to @var{DIR}.  So the bundle opens the same on any machine,
and it carries no trace of the one it was made on --- an absolute path names a home
directory, and a directory you hand to somebody should not name yours.

Nothing is copied twice: several faces out of one file share its copy, and two fonts
that happen to be called @file{Regular.ttf} get distinct names in @file{fonts/}.
fontina refuses to write into a directory that already holds a bundle rather than mix
two collections' fonts together.
@item fontina collection import FILE|DIR
Read one back, matching faces against this index by identity hash, then PostScript name,
then path.  Naming a bundle directory is enough; its relative paths are joined onto it.
Faces this index does not have are reported as missing rather than invented.
@end table

@noindent
A bundle is the shareable form, and it is deliberately plain: a directory of fonts and
one JSON file with a published schema.  You do not need @command{fontina} to open it, or
our permission to write one.

@node Freedom
@chapter Which of your fonts are free software

A font is a work of practical use.  The question of whether you may study it, change it
and pass it on is the same question that applies to a program, and it deserves the same
answer: yes or no, with a reason.

Most font managers will tell you a font's file size and its designer.  Very few will
tell you whether you are allowed to modify it.  @command{fontina} does.  It reads the
license the font declares in @code{name} IDs 13 and 14, maps it to an SPDX identifier,
and judges that identifier against the FSF's list of free licenses.

@table @code
@item free
The license grants all four freedoms: to run the font, to study it, to change it, and to
redistribute it, changed or not.
@item nonfree
The license withholds at least one of them.
@item unknown
A license is stated, but it is not one that is known to be free.  The program says so
and gives its reason rather than guessing; read the license yourself.
@item unstated
The font carries no license information at all.  No permission is stated, so in law none
is granted, however the font may have been meant to be shared.
@end table

@example
fontina list --free              # only the fonts you may build on
fontina list --freedom unknown   # the ones worth reading the license of
fontina license                  # every verdict, with its reason
fontina facets                   # how much of the library is free
@end example

In @code{fontina list}, the fifth character of the @code{flags} column carries the same
verdict: @code{F} free, @code{N} nonfree, @code{?} unknown, @code{-} unstated.

@section On fsType

@code{OS/2.fsType} is a set of bits in which a font file states what it would prefer
other programs to allow when embedding it.  It is not a term of any license, and it is
not enforceable by the file on the machine reading it.

@command{fontina} reports those bits and never acts on them.  A font marked
@dfn{Restricted License} still parses, still indexes, still previews and still exports.
The health check @code{license/embedding} notes the value and says explicitly that it is
reported and not enforced.  Treating a flag inside a data file as permission to restrict
the person running the program would be the wrong way round.

@node Data you own
@chapter Where things are kept

@table @asis
@item The index
One SQLite file.  On GNU/Linux, @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/fontina/index.db}; elsewhere, the
platform data directory.  @env{FONTINA_DB} or @option{--db} puts it anywhere you like.
It holds no original data: everything in it was read from your font files, and
@code{fontina scan} rebuilds it from disk in seconds.

@item Exports
JSON, validated against the schemas in @file{schemas/}.  @code{fontina schema} prints
them.

@item Configuration
TOML.

@item Activation, on GNU/Linux
Symlinks under @file{$XDG_DATA_HOME/fonts}, declared to fontconfig in
@file{~/.config/fontconfig/conf.d}.  Nothing that needs @command{fontina} to
understand.
@end table

@node Reporting bugs
@chapter Reporting bugs

Report bugs on the project's issue tracker.  A useful report says what you ran, what you
expected, and what happened; if a font file is involved, say what it is and whether its
license permits you to attach it.

Patches are welcome under the GNU General Public License version 3 or later.  There is no
contributor licence agreement and no copyright assignment: you keep your copyright.

